// StrengthReduceDivision — a Std→Std pass that replaces `x / K` and `x mod K`, for a COMPILE-TIME
// constant `K`, with a multiply-and-shift sequence that computes the same answer without a divide
// (docs/emitted-code-roadmap.md).
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHY THIS ROW EXISTS AT ALL: `idiv` IS THE MOST EXPENSIVE INSTRUCTION THE COMPILER EMITS.** A 64-bit
// x64 `idiv` is 20–40 cycles and is not pipelined; the sequence below is one `imul` (3 cycles) and
// three or four single-cycle ALU ops. MEASURED on the committed corpus: 22 divides,
// of which the ones a program actually PAYS are in the compiler's float-formatting stdlib (`__decimalWidthOf`
// and `__appendDecimalGroup` divide by 10 four times per number) and in `graphemeBreakProperty`
// (`mod 28` per grapheme). Neither benchmark's hot loop contains one, which is why the row's probe is
// `temp/codegen-probe/fmt.maxon` rather than nbody or fannkuch.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS A Std PASS AND NOT AN INSTRUCTION SELECTION, AND THAT IS FORCED RATHER THAN CHOSEN.**
// A transform whose CONSTRAINT is x64's belongs at x64 isel. This one
// cannot go there: the sequence needs four or five INTERMEDIATE VALUES, and **the isel cannot mint a
// vreg** — every `TargetVReg.virtual(id)` names a Std `ValueId`, the parser owns that value space
// (`IrFunction.valueTags` is dense by ValueId) and the class column is derived from the Std module
// (`buildFunctionValueClasses`), so a backend-minted vreg would be filled `gpr` by accident rather than
// by decision. `TargetDialect.absF64RegReg` and `StdToX64Conversion.materializeFloatEquality` both
// record the same wall from the other side. At the Std tier minting is ordinary (`ValueMinter`), and
// three later passes then read the sequence as the arithmetic it is:
//
//   • `foldConstOperands` turns every constant operand into an IMMEDIATE, so the shift counts and the
//     multiply-back need no register (`sar rax, 3`, `imul rcx, rcx, 10`).
//   • `commonSubexpressionElimination` MERGES the quotient chain of `x / K` with that of `x mod K` in
//     the same block, which no isel-tier rewrite could ever reach: two `idiv`s can never be CSE'd,
//     because they are `isPure: false`. ⚠ **MEASURED, AND IT HOLDS FOR ONE OF THE TWO SHAPES ONLY.**
//     `(n / 8) + (n mod 8)` emits the four-op quotient chain ONCE and the remainder reuses it; the same
//     program over `10` emits the chain TWICE. The reason is CSE's filed one — there is no constant
//     interning, so the two sites mint two `const` ops for one 64-bit magic, the two `mulHighSigned`s
//     read different value ids and no expression comparison can call them equal. A power-of-two chain
//     has no such constant left after `foldConstOperands` (every operand is an immediate), which is
//     exactly why it merges. ⇒ `const` unification would buy this row a second time.
//   • `loopInvariantCodeMotion` can hoist a reduced quotient out of a loop. An `idiv` never left one.
//
// ⚠ **THE TARGET GATE.** The general sequence needs a 64×64 → 128 multiply, of which it reads the HIGH
// half (`StdBinOpcode.mulHighSigned` / `mulHighUnsigned`, one per signedness — the high half is the one
// place the two differ). x64 has both (`imul r/m64`, `mul r/m64`) and arm64 has both (`SMULH`, `UMULH`);
// **wasm32 has NONE** — emulating it costs four 32×32 products and their carries, ~20 instructions
// against the ONE `i64.div_s` the reduction would be replacing. So the pass asks
// `targetLowersMulHigh` and rewrites NOTHING on a target that cannot spell the opcode.
// `PromoteStackRecords` is the precedent for a target-conditional Std pass, and the gate is what keeps
// every wasm golden byte-identical for this row.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE DIVISORS THIS PASS REFUSES, AND EACH REFUSAL IS A CORRECTNESS ARGUMENT RATHER THAN A
// MISSING FEATURE.** `StdOp.div`/`mod` carry `isPure: false` because x64's `idiv` FAULTS, and
// replacing one with pure arithmetic DELETES that fault. That is sound only where the fault provably
// cannot happen, which is what these refusals establish (the last one is about something else again —
// it is the only one that prevents a wrong ANSWER rather than a deleted trap):
//
//   • **`K == 0`.** The fault IS the answer. `Parser.emitDivOrMod` refuses a provably-zero divisor in
//     source (E3103), and a compiler-authored builder that divides by a literal zero is a bug the
//     `#DE` reports; reducing it would silently compute a number instead.
//   • **`|K| == 1`.** `x / 1` is `x` and `x mod 1` is `0` — an ALGEBRAIC IDENTITY, not a strength
//     reduction, and it belongs to `foldConstOperands`' identity move (which cannot reach it today
//     because `div`/`mod` have no `binOpImm` form). `x / -1` additionally FAULTS at `i64.min`, and
//     `specs/division.md` pins that fault as deliberate — a reduction would answer `i64.min`.
//     Refusing the whole magnitude keeps one rule where two would need to agree.
//   • **`K == i64.min`.** The derivation works in `|K|`, which is not representable.
//   • **UNSIGNED by a divisor at or above `2^63`** — i.e. one whose `ParsedInt` is NEGATIVE. This is
//     the only refusal here that prevents a WRONG answer rather than an unsound trap deletion; see
//     `classifyDivision`, which states it where the magnitude is taken.
//
// ⇒ For every divisor that IS reduced, `2 <= |K| <= i64.max`, so the `idiv` it replaces could not have
// faulted on either operand — the trap is removed along with the only inputs that could raise it.
//
// ⚠ **THE ANSWER MUST AGREE WITH `idiv` ON EVERY EDGE, NOT APPROXIMATELY.** Signed division TRUNCATES
// TOWARD ZERO, which neither the magic multiply nor an arithmetic shift does on its own: `x / 2` for a
// negative `x` is **not** `x >> 1`. Both SIGNED sequences below therefore carry an explicit sign
// correction — an unsigned dividend has no negative half to correct and carries none —
// and `specs/strength-reduction.md` is a DIFFERENTIAL test rather than a table of expected values
// — it divides by a constant and by a runtime value of the same magnitude and asserts the two agree,
// so the hardware `idiv` is the oracle for every case, over `i64.min`, `i64.max`, `±1`, `0`, both
// signs of dividend against both signs of divisor, every power of two and a spread of odd divisors.

// The Std type every value in the emitted sequence carries. An integer `binOp` computes at the MACHINE
// WORD on every target the compiler emits for (`foldConstants`' header states the argument in full), so a Std
// `operandType` is a STORAGE class here and never an arithmetic one — and the divide this replaces was
// itself a full-width `idiv` of whatever the register held, whatever the source type's width.
let ReducedOperandType = StdType.i64

// The bit position of a 64-bit value's SIGN, used twice: to broadcast it (`x shr 63` is all-ones for a
// negative `x`) and to isolate it (`x shrLogical 63` is 0 or 1).
let SignBitIndex = 63

// The number of bits in the machine word the sequence computes at.
let ReducedWordBits = 64

let AddIndicatorHalvingShift = 1

// The largest `q1` the magic refinement can hold and still DOUBLE without leaving i64 — see
// `deriveSignedMagic`, whose one decline is this bound. It is `floor(i64.max / 2)`, spelled as a SHIFT
// because a literal `/ 2` at file scope is folded in the FLOAT domain and the comparison against a
// `ParsedInt` then does not type-check.
let MaxRefinableQuotient = i64.max shr 1

// The refinement provably converges by `p = 2 * 64 - 1` (Granlund–Montgomery), i.e. within 64 steps of
// its `p = 63` seed. The loop is written with that bound so a derivation that did NOT converge is a
// panic naming the divisor rather than a hang.
let MaxMagicRefinements = 64

// The modulus `divideTwoToThe63` divides by. ⭐ **THE RANGE IS THE PRECONDITION, DECLARED RATHER THAN
// CHECKED BY HAND**: `2^63 / m` needs `m` non-zero, and a `ParsedInt` parameter would make that divide
// FALLIBLE (E3103's runtime twin — `Parser.emitDivOrMod` requires a `try` for a possibly-zero divisor).
// Declaring the narrower type makes the divide total, and the cast at each call site is where the
// caller's own proof is checked — at compile time where the compiler can see it, and by a range guard
// where it cannot.
typealias PositiveModulus = int(1 to i64.max)

// Whether this CPU's instruction selection lowers `StdBinOpcode.mulHighSigned`.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A QUESTION ABOUT THE LOWERING, NOT ABOUT THE HARDWARE, and wasm32 is the one that shows
// why.** Both CPUs have the instruction — x64's fixed-register `imul r/m64` and AArch64's three-address
// `SMULH Xd, Xn, Xm` — and each has a `TargetOp` and an isel arm for its own spelling. wasm32's `false`
// is the hardware's: there is no `i64.mul_high_s`, and building one costs four 32×32 products and their
// carries against the single `i64.div_s` the reduction would replace.
//
// wasm's isel PANICS on the opcode it cannot lower, so a wrong answer here is a loud compiler failure on
// the first reduced division rather than a silent miscompile.
module function targetLowersMulHigh(cpu CpuArch) returns bool
	return match cpu 'c'
		x64 or
			arm64 gives true
		wasm32 gives false
	end 'c'
end 'targetLowersMulHigh'

// Which shape a division by a constant reduces to. An enum field of a record rather than a
// payload-bearing union, for `StdArithOperands`' measured reason: a union heap-boxes every case,
// payload-free ones included.
enum DivisionReductionKind
	// Keep the `div`/`mod` exactly as it is — see the four refusals in this file's header.
	notReducible

	// `|K|` is `2^shift`: an arithmetic shift with a bias that makes it truncate toward zero (signed),
	// or a plain logical shift / mask (unsigned).
	powerOfTwo

	// The general case: multiply by `magic` and take the high half, then the tail its signedness owes —
	// a shift and the sign bit (signed), or the round-up fixup and a logical shift (unsigned).
	magicMultiply
end 'DivisionReductionKind'

// What one `div`/`mod` op reduces to, written into a caller-owned record refilled per site.
//
// ⚠ **EVERY FIELD BELOW IS MEANINGLESS UNTIL `kind` SAYS WHICH OF THEM THE LAST CLASSIFICATION FILLED**
// — `StdArithOperands`' discipline, and for its reason: 0 is a real ValueId, a real constant and a real
// shift, so no field here can be read as a sentinel.
module type DivisionReduction
	export var kind as DivisionReductionKind

	// `|K|`, always in `2 .. i64.max` when `kind` is not `notReducible`.
	export var divisorMagnitude as ParsedInt

	// Whether the emitted QUOTIENT must be negated. True exactly for a `/` by a NEGATIVE constant: the
	// sequences below compute `x / |K|`, and truncating division is odd, so `x / K` is `-(x / |K|)`.
	// A `mod` never negates — a truncating remainder takes the DIVIDEND's sign and its magnitude is
	// `|x| mod |K|`, so the divisor's sign cannot reach it.
	//
	// ⚠ The negation cannot overflow, which is what makes this identity exact rather than nearly so:
	// `|K| >= 2`, so `|x / |K||` is at most `2^62` and its negative is representable.
	export var negateQuotient as bool

	// `powerOfTwo`: `log2(|K|)`. `magicMultiply`: the right shift the magic's fixed-point scale needs,
	// which may be 0 (in which case no shift op is emitted at all).
	export var shift as ShiftCount

	// `magicMultiply` only: the multiplier, read as a SIGNED 64-bit number.
	export var magic as ParsedInt

	// `magicMultiply` only: whether the magic's true (unsigned) value was at least `2^63`, i.e. whether
	// the signed `magic` above came out negative. The sequence then adds the dividend back — see
	// `emitMagicQuotient`.
	export var magicIsNegative as bool

	// Whether the division reads its operands as UNSIGNED. It selects the tail, not just the opcode: an
	// unsigned dividend has no negative half, so no sequence emitted for one carries a sign correction.
	export var isUnsigned as bool

	// `magicMultiply`, unsigned only: whether `deriveUnsignedMagic` had to round the reciprocal up past
	// 64 bits, so the stored multiplier is the true one MINUS `2^64` and the sequence owes the fixup that
	// adds that missing bit back (`emitUnsignedMagicTail`). It is not the signed `magicIsNegative`, which
	// is about how the machine READS a stored multiplier and is derived rather than recorded.
	export var addIndicator as bool

	module static function create() returns DivisionReduction
		return Self{kind: DivisionReductionKind.notReducible, divisorMagnitude: 0, negateQuotient: false, shift: 0, magic: 0, magicIsNegative: false, isUnsigned: false, addIndicator: false}
	end 'create'

	function recordNotReducible()
		self.kind = DivisionReductionKind.notReducible
	end 'recordNotReducible'

	function recordPowerOfTwo(magnitude ParsedInt, negate bool, unsigned bool)
		self.kind = DivisionReductionKind.powerOfTwo
		self.divisorMagnitude = magnitude
		self.shift = log2OfPowerOfTwo(magnitude)
		self.negateQuotient = negate
		self.isUnsigned = unsigned
		self.magic = 0
		self.magicIsNegative = false
		self.addIndicator = false
	end 'recordPowerOfTwo'

	function recordMagic(magnitude ParsedInt, multiplier ParsedInt, postShift ShiftCount, negate bool, unsigned bool, carriesImplicitBit bool)
		self.kind = DivisionReductionKind.magicMultiply
		self.divisorMagnitude = magnitude
		self.magic = multiplier
		self.magicIsNegative = multiplier < 0
		self.shift = postShift
		self.negateQuotient = negate
		self.isUnsigned = unsigned
		self.addIndicator = carriesImplicitBit
	end 'recordMagic'

	function isReducible() returns bool
		return self.kind != DivisionReductionKind.notReducible
	end 'isReducible'
end 'DivisionReduction'

// The `(multiplier, postShift)` pair either derivation answers with, plus whether it found one at all.
// Caller-owned and refilled, for `DivisionReduction`'s reason.
//
// ⚠ `found` is the SIGNED derivation's alone, and `classifyDivision` reads it only there. The signed
// derivation SEARCHES for the scale and can run out of signed range on the way; the unsigned one takes
// a fixed number of doublings and always answers, with the 65th bit carried by `addIndicator`.
module type DivisionMagic
	export var found as bool
	export var multiplier as ParsedInt
	export var postShift as ShiftCount
	export var addIndicator as bool

	module static function create() returns DivisionMagic
		return Self{found: false, multiplier: 0, postShift: 0, addIndicator: false}
	end 'create'
end 'DivisionMagic'

// `floor(2^p / m)` and `2^p mod m` for a `p` the caller raises one bit at a time — the shared state of
// both derivations, seeded at `p = 63` by `divideTwoToThe63`.
type FixedPointRatio
	export var quotient as ParsedInt
	export var remainder as ParsedInt

	static function seeded(seedQuotient ParsedInt, seedRemainder ParsedInt) returns FixedPointRatio
		return Self{quotient: seedQuotient, remainder: seedRemainder}
	end 'seeded'

	// ⚠ The carry is tested as `r >= m - r` and applied as `r - (m - r)` rather than as the `2r >= m` /
	// `2r - m` the arithmetic reads as. `r` and `m` are both below `2^63` but `2r` need not be, and a
	// `ParsedInt` that wraps past `i64.max` compares NEGATIVE — which would take the wrong branch and
	// answer a plausible, wrong magic. Only `quotient` is allowed to wrap here.
	function raiseByOneBit(modulus ParsedInt)
		if self.remainder >= modulus - self.remainder 'carry'
			self.quotient = self.quotient * 2 + 1
			self.remainder = self.remainder - (modulus - self.remainder)
			return
		end 'carry'

		self.quotient = self.quotient * 2
		self.remainder = self.remainder + self.remainder
	end 'raiseByOneBit'
end 'FixedPointRatio'

// ==================================================================================================
// The derivation
// ==================================================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **THE MAGIC NUMBER, DERIVED RATHER THAN TABULATED.** This is Granlund & Montgomery's algorithm
// ("Division by Invariant Integers using Multiplication", PLDI 1994), in the form Hacker's Delight
// gives as figure 10-1 `magic()`, widened from 32 to 64 bits. It answers, for a divisor `d >= 2`, a
// pair `(M, s)` such that for EVERY 64-bit signed dividend `n`:
//
//     n / d  ==  (mulhi(n, M) [+ n if M < 0]) shr s  +  the sign bit of that
//
// **WHAT THE LOOP IS.** `q1`/`r1` track `floor(2^p / anc)` and `2^p mod anc`; `q2`/`r2` track the same
// two for `d`. Each iteration raises `p` by one, which is one doubling with a remainder correction —
// no division at all is performed after the two seeds. `anc` is the largest positive `n` that is
// `-1 (mod d)`, i.e. the dividend at which the approximation is hardest, and the loop's exit condition
// IS the proof obligation — so the FIRST `p` that satisfies it gives the smallest exact multiplier.
// ⇒ **a reader checks this routine by checking that it transcribes that condition, and then by running
// `specs/strength-reduction.md`, which divides by these constants and by a runtime divisor of the
// same value and asserts the two agree.**
//
// ⚠⚠ **ONE VALUE IS ALLOWED TO WRAP AND EVERY OTHER ONE MUST NOT, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT MAKES
// THIS EXPRESSIBLE IN A LANGUAGE WITH NO u64 ARITHMETIC.** The published algorithm computes in UNSIGNED
// 64-bit; a `ParsedInt` is SIGNED 64-bit and the compiler's comparisons are signed, so a value above `2^63`
// would compare as negative and the loop would take the wrong branch. Term by term:
//
//   • `d`, `anc`, `r1`, `r2`, `delta` are remainders or magnitudes below `2^63` and are EXACT. The
//     doublings are written `r >= m - r` / `r - (m - r)` rather than `2*r >= m` / `2*r - m` so that no
//     intermediate leaves the range either.
//   • `q1` must be exact, because the exit condition COMPARES it. It stays below `2^63` for every
//     divisor the loop converges on — but not for every divisor: MEASURED, it first exceeds i64 for
//     divisors around `2^62.5`, and for none below `2^61` at all. The routine tests the bound BEFORE
//     each doubling and answers `found: false`, which is the fifth refusal and the only one that is
//     about this file's arithmetic rather than about the language's.
//   • `q2` is the ONLY term allowed to wrap, and its wrapped value is precisely the answer wanted:
//     `M = q2 + 1` is a 64-bit multiplier whose top bit may legitimately be set (that is what
//     `magicIsNegative` and the `+ n` fixup are for), and `+` and `*2` are exact modulo 2^64, so the
//     signed value computed here IS the bit pattern the instruction must hold.
//
// ⚠ `2^63` itself is not representable, so the two seeds are computed through `i64.max` with the
// missing `+ 1` carried by hand — see `divideTwoToThe63`.
//
// ⭐ **THE DIVISOR IS ALWAYS POSITIVE HERE (`|K|`), AND THAT SIMPLIFICATION IS LOAD-BEARING.** The
// published algorithm handles a negative `d` by biasing `anc` and negating `M`; working in `|K|` and
// negating the QUOTIENT instead is exact (truncating division is odd) and removes the one case in
// which `anc` reaches `2^63` and stops fitting — a negative `d` whose magnitude divides `2^63 + 1`,
// of which `x / -3` is the smallest.
function deriveSignedMagic(divisor ParsedInt, out DivisionMagic)
	if divisor < 2 'notADerivableDivisor'
		panic("deriveSignedMagic: divisor {divisor} — the derivation is defined for a magnitude of 2 or more, and `classifyDivision` refuses 0, 1 and i64.min before asking")
	end 'notADerivableDivisor'

	out.found = false

	// `anc` = 2^63 - 1 - (2^63 mod divisor): the largest n below 2^63 that is -1 modulo the divisor.
	var divisorRatio = divideTwoToThe63(divisor as PositiveModulus)
	let anc = i64.max - divisorRatio.remainder

	var ancRatio = divideTwoToThe63(anc as PositiveModulus)

	var p = SignBitIndex

	for _ in 0 upto MaxMagicRefinements 'refine'
		p = p + 1

		if ancRatio.quotient > MaxRefinableQuotient 'wouldLeaveTheSignedRange'
			return
		end 'wouldLeaveTheSignedRange'

		// q1 = floor(2^p / anc), r1 = 2^p mod anc — one doubling with a remainder correction.
		ancRatio.raiseByOneBit(anc)

		// The same two for the divisor. ⚠ `q2` is the term that may WRAP — see the header.
		divisorRatio.raiseByOneBit(divisor)

		let delta = divisor - divisorRatio.remainder

		// ⚠ Hacker's Delight writes this as a do-WHILE, so the published expression is the CONTINUE
		// condition and the loop stops when it stops holding. Transcribing it as an EXIT condition is
		// the one transcription error this routine can make and the one a reader should check for:
		// with the sense inverted, `x / 3` derives no magic at all and the refinement runs away.
		let keepRefining = ancRatio.quotient < delta or (ancRatio.quotient == delta and ancRatio.remainder == 0)

		if not keepRefining 'converged'
			out.found = true
			out.multiplier = divisorRatio.quotient + 1
			out.postShift = (p - ReducedWordBits) as ShiftCount
			return
		end 'converged'
	end 'refine'

	panic("deriveSignedMagic: the refinement for divisor {divisor} did not converge within {MaxMagicRefinements} steps — Granlund-Montgomery proves it converges by p = 2*64 - 1, so either the exit condition or one of the doublings has been transcribed wrongly")
end 'deriveSignedMagic'

// ⭐ **THE UNSIGNED RECIPROCAL, IN THE ROUND-UP FORM (libdivide's `u64_gen`) AND NOT HACKER'S DELIGHT
// FIG 10-2 — because a `ParsedInt` CANNOT DERIVE FIG 10-2 AT ALL.** That figure's track runs through
// `nc = 2^64 - 1 - (2^64 mod d)`, which exceeds `2^63` for every admissible `d`, and its `r1` ranges
// over `[0, nc)`; every comparison on either is an UNSIGNED one, and this compiler's arithmetic is
// signed 64-bit throughout. The round-up form needs only `floor(2^(64+s)/d)` and its remainder, where
// `r < d < 2^63` holds at every step, so the same discipline the file header states for the signed `q2`
// carries the whole derivation: the quotient is the one term allowed to wrap.
//
// `s` is `floor(log2 K)` and is fixed rather than searched, which is why there is no decline. When the
// rounded-up reciprocal needs a 65th bit, the multiplier stored is that value minus `2^64` and
// `addIndicator` says the sequence owes the fixup that restores it.
function deriveUnsignedMagic(divisor ParsedInt, out DivisionMagic)
	if divisor < 2 or isPowerOfTwo(divisor) 'notADerivableDivisor'
		panic("deriveUnsignedMagic: divisor {divisor} — the derivation is defined for a magnitude of 2 or more that is NOT a power of two, and `classifyDivision` refuses 0, 1 and i64.min and takes every power of two before asking")
	end 'notADerivableDivisor'

	let scale = floorLog2(divisor)

	var ratio = divideTwoToThe63(divisor as PositiveModulus)

	for _ in 0 to scale 'raiseToTheMagicScale'
		ratio.raiseByOneBit(divisor)
	end 'raiseToTheMagicScale'

	out.found = true
	out.postShift = scale

	let roundUpBound = (1 as ParsedInt) shl (scale as ParsedInt)

	if divisor - ratio.remainder < roundUpBound 'roundUpFitsSixtyFourBits'
		out.multiplier = ratio.quotient + 1
		out.addIndicator = false
		return
	end 'roundUpFitsSixtyFourBits'

	ratio.raiseByOneBit(divisor)
	out.multiplier = ratio.quotient + 1
	out.addIndicator = true
end 'deriveUnsignedMagic'

// `floor(2^63 / m)` and `2^63 mod m`, for `1 <= m <= i64.max`, in SIGNED 64-bit arithmetic alone.
//
// ⚠ `2^63` is one past `i64.max` and cannot be written down here at all, so it is spelled `i64.max + 1`
// and the `+ 1` is carried through the division by hand: `i64.max = q0*m + r0` gives
// `2^63 = q0*m + (r0 + 1)`, which is already the answer unless the incremented remainder has reached
// `m`, in which case one more `m` comes out of it. Both outputs are written into caller-supplied
// variables because these two are one division and a function answers with one value.
//
// ⚠ `m` is a `PositiveModulus` rather than a `ParsedInt`, which is what makes the two divides below
// TOTAL — see that typealias. Both call sites pass a magnitude the classifier has already proven to be
// at least 2, or an `anc` derived from one.
function divideTwoToThe63(m PositiveModulus) returns FixedPointRatio
	let modulus = m as ParsedInt
	let q0 = (i64.max / m) as ParsedInt
	let r0 = i64.max - q0 * modulus

	if r0 + 1 == modulus 'theIncrementCompletesOneMoreDivisor'
		return FixedPointRatio.seeded(q0 + 1, seedRemainder: 0)
	end 'theIncrementCompletesOneMoreDivisor'

	return FixedPointRatio.seeded(q0, seedRemainder: r0 + 1)
end 'divideTwoToThe63'

// ⭐ **THE ONE ADMISSION RULE — what one `div`/`mod` site reduces to, written into `out`.** Every
// refusal in this file's header is decided here and nowhere else, so the emitters below may assume
// `2 <= divisorMagnitude <= i64.max` unconditionally.
function classifyDivision(divisor ParsedInt, signedness IntegerSignedness, isRemainder bool, magic DivisionMagic, out DivisionReduction)
	out.recordNotReducible()

	// `0` keeps its fault; `i64.min`'s magnitude is not representable; `|K| == 1` is an identity rather
	// than a strength reduction, and `x / -1` must keep faulting at `i64.min`. See the header.
	if divisor == 0 or divisor == i64.min or divisor == 1 or divisor == -1 'refusedDivisor'
		return
	end 'refusedDivisor'

	let unsigned = signedness == IntegerSignedness.unsigned

	// ⛔⛔ **THE ONE PLACE THE TWO SIGNEDNESSES DISAGREE ABOUT WHAT THE SAME 64 BITS MEAN, AND READING
	// IT WRONG IS A WRONG ANSWER RATHER THAN A MISSED REDUCTION.** `magnitude` below is `|K|` read as a
	// SIGNED number; for an UNSIGNED divide, a `K` with its top bit set is not a small negative number
	// at all but a value of at least `2^63`. `x /u 0xFFFF_FFFF_FFFF_FFF0` is `0` for every dividend
	// below `2^64 - 16`, while its signed magnitude is `16` — a power of two, which would have answered
	// `x shrLogical 4`. It IS reachable from source: a `let` whose declared type is `int(0 to u64.max)`
	// and whose folded value wraps past `i64.max` is an unsigned divisor by both operands' declared
	// types and a negative `ParsedInt` here.
	//
	// ⇒ Refused outright. Every such divisor is at least `2^63`, so `x /u K` is `0` or `1` and
	// `x modu K` is `x` or `x - K` — three shapes worth their own pass if one ever pays, and none of
	// them is what the sequences below compute. `specs/strength-reduction.md`'s
	// `an-unsigned-divisor-above-the-signed-range-is-refused` is the pin.
	if unsigned and divisor < 0 'unsignedDivisorAboveTheSignedRange'
		return
	end 'unsignedDivisorAboveTheSignedRange'

	let magnitude = divisor if divisor > 0 else -divisor

	// A `mod` never negates: a truncating remainder's magnitude is `|x| mod |K|` and its sign is the
	// DIVIDEND's, so the divisor's sign cannot reach the answer.
	//
	// ⚠ There is deliberately no `and not unsigned` here, though it would read as the safer spelling:
	// an unsigned divisor is POSITIVE by the refusal directly above, so the two conditions would be one
	// fact stated twice — and the copy that went stale would be this one.
	let negate = divisor < 0 and not isRemainder

	if isPowerOfTwo(magnitude) 'shiftSequence'
		out.recordPowerOfTwo(magnitude, negate: negate, unsigned: unsigned)
		return
	end 'shiftSequence'

	if unsigned 'unsignedMagic'
		deriveUnsignedMagic(magnitude, out: magic)
		out.recordMagic(magnitude, multiplier: magic.multiplier, postShift: magic.postShift, negate: negate, unsigned: true, carriesImplicitBit: magic.addIndicator)
		return
	end 'unsignedMagic'

	deriveSignedMagic(magnitude, out: magic)

	if not magic.found 'outsideTheDerivableRange'
		return
	end 'outsideTheDerivableRange'

	out.recordMagic(magnitude, multiplier: magic.multiplier, postShift: magic.postShift, negate: negate, unsigned: false, carriesImplicitBit: magic.addIndicator)
end 'classifyDivision'

// ==================================================================================================
// The pass
// ==================================================================================================

// The pass's headline: what it is about to do, or why it does nothing on this target. `reduces` is
// `targetLowersMulHigh`'s answer for the compile's CPU.
module function strengthReduceDivisionHeadline(reduces bool, firstFuncIndex FuncIndex, funcCount FuncCount) returns String
	if not reduces 'targetCannotSpellTheMultiply'
		return "strengthReduceDivision: skipped — this target lowers neither `mulHighSigned` nor `mulHighUnsigned`"
	end 'targetCannotSpellTheMultiply'

	return "strengthReduceDivision: function {firstFuncIndex} onward of {funcCount}"
end 'strengthReduceDivisionHeadline'

// ⚠ **THE PRE-SCAN IS A SECOND DESCENT AND IT IS SAFE FOR A STATED REASON**, which any pass that
// walks its ops twice owes: it asks a STRICTLY WEAKER question than the rewrite —
// "is there a `div` or a `mod` here at all?" against "is this one reducible?" — so the only
// disagreement it can have is to admit a function the rewrite then changes nothing in, which costs one
// walk. It CANNOT skip a site the rewrite would have taken, because the reducible sites are a subset of
// the divisions. What it buys is everything below it: most functions in a program contain no division
// at all, and `liveOpIndices`, the const map, the value-id mint and the per-block `opRefs` rebuild are
// all allocations they would otherwise pay for nothing.
module function reduceDivisionsInFunc(module StdModule, func IrFunction, reduction DivisionReduction, magic DivisionMagic, def StdFoldableDef)
	if not funcHoldsADivision(module, func: func) 'noDivision'
		return
	end 'noDivision'

	let opIndices = module.liveOpIndices(func)

	// ⚠ A `ConstValueMap` does not partition by DOMAIN (an integer `const`'s payload is a number and a
	// float's is an IEEE bit pattern), and this pass needs no partition: `StdOp.div`/`mod` are INTEGER
	// division — float division is `binOp(div)`, which never reaches this pass — so a divisor read out
	// of this map is an integer by construction.
	let constMap = collectConstValues(module, opIndices: opIndices, def: def)

	var mint = ValueMinter.resuming(nextFreeValueId(module, func: func))

	// The divisor `const`s of every site this pass rewrote. A rewritten division reads its divisor
	// NOWHERE — the magic and the multiply-back are fresh constants — so such a `const` is left with
	// one fewer reader, and is dead when it had no other. Same narrow rule `foldConstOperands` owns
	// (retire a `const` nothing reads); membership is re-derived from the REWRITTEN function below
	// rather than counted here, which is exact and needs no use census of its own. A LIST rather than
	// a `StdValueUseSet` because this one is ITERATED, which that dense column has no operation for —
	// and a repeated id costs one redundant `contains` at the end of the function, nothing more.
	var detachedDivisors = ValueIdArray.create()

	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		var block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("reduceDivisionsInFunc: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
		var rebuilt = OpIndexArray.create()

		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachRef'
			appendReducedOp(module, opIdx: opIdx, constMap: constMap, reduction: reduction, magic: magic, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt, detached: detachedDivisors)
		end 'eachRef'

		block.opRefs = rebuilt
	end 'eachBlock'

	retireDetachedDivisors(module, func: func, detached: detachedDivisors)
end 'reduceDivisionsInFunc'

// Whether `func` holds a `div` or a `mod` op at all — the pre-scan the function above states the
// argument for. It walks `func.blockRefs` → `block.opRefs` directly rather than through
// `liveOpIndices`, which is the point: that call ALLOCATES an array per function.
//
// ⚠ A TERMINATOR IS NEVER A DIVISION (`div`/`mod` are body ops), so `opRefs` reaches every site — the
// same argument `PromoteStackRecords.applyPromotions` makes for its own rebuild.
function funcHoldsADivision(module StdModule, func IrFunction) returns bool
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("funcHoldsADivision: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachRef'
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("funcHoldsADivision: opIdx {opIdx} from block.opRefs not in module.ops")

			if opIsIntegerDivision(op) 'found'
				return true
			end 'found'
		end 'eachRef'
	end 'eachBlock'

	return false
end 'funcHoldsADivision'

// Whether `op` is one of the two INTEGER division variants. Exhaustive over `StdOp` with no bare
// `default`, so a variant appended at the end of the union is a compile error here rather than an op
// this pre-scan silently cannot see.
function opIsIntegerDivision(op StdOp) returns bool
	return match op 'k'
		const or
			binOp or
			unaryOp or
			cmp or
			binOpImm or
			cmpImm or
			condBranch or
			branch or
			ret or
			param or
			call or
			retVoid or
			globalAddr or
			loadIndirect or
			storeIndirect or
			osAllocPages or
			osWriteStdout or
			rdataAddr or
			tryCall or
			errorReturn or
			osExit or
			funcAddr or
			callIndirect or
			osPanic or
			osReadClock or
			osProcessSpawn or
			osProcessExitCode or
			osCloseHandle or
			osWaitHandle or
			osWaitChild or
			osFreePages or
			switch or
			witnessCall or
			osPipeCreate or
			osEventCreate or
			osThreadCreate or
			osLockInit or
			osLockEnter or
			osLockLeave or
			osReadOverlapped or
			osLastError or
			osProcessSpawnInherit or
			osCancelIo or
			osGetPid or
			osPipeCreateOutbound or
			osWriteFile or
			osReadWallClock or
			witnessTryCall or
			osFileOpenRead or
			osFileClose or
			osReadFile or
			osFileOpenWrite or
			osFileSize or
			osFileDelete or
			osFileRename or
			osFileFindFirst or
			osFileFindClose or
			osFileFindNext or
			osDirCreate or
			osDirRemove or
			osDirCurrentPath or
			osFileAttributes or
			osPanicValue or
			osCommandLine or
			osStdHandle or
			osExecutablePath or
			osWriteStderr or
			stackRecordAddr or
			osOpenNamedSegment or
			osMapNamedSegment or
			osTickCountMs or
			osHandleSetInherit or
			osPipePeek or
			osEnvRead or
			osProcessTerminate or
			osSocketStartup or
			osSocketResolve or
			osSocketFreeAddrInfo or
			osSocketCreate or
			osSocketConnect or
			osSocketSend or
			osSocketError or
			osSocketRecv or
			osSocketClose or
			osCpuCount or
			osThreadCpuTicks or
			osDecommitPages or
			memFill or
			osCommitPages or
			osReservePages or
			atomicCas or
			atomicRmw or
			currentThreadToken or
			tlsAlloc or
			tlsSetValue or
			tlsSlotLoad or
			osSetEvent or
			osEnterBackgroundPriority or
			osFileOpenWriteExecutable or
			osEnvBlock or
			osEnvBlockFree or
			loadAcquire or
			storeRelease or
			osHandleFileType or
			osProcessProbe or
			osCreateNamedSegment or
			osUnmapNamedSegment or
			osUnpublishNamedSegment or
			covPoint or
			osHostSample or
			osNetpoll or
			osPathQuery or
			osSocketOverlapped or
			osSocketServe or
			osDebugHost gives false
		div or
			mod gives true
	end 'k'
end 'opIsIntegerDivision'

// Append the ops that belong at `opIdx` — the original op, or the sequence a reducible division
// becomes.
function appendReducedOp(module StdModule, opIdx OpIndex, constMap ConstValueMap, reduction DivisionReduction, magic DivisionMagic, mint ValueMinter, rebuilt OpIndexArray, detached ValueIdArray)
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("appendReducedOp: opIdx {opIdx} from block.opRefs not in module.ops")

	match op 'site'
		const or
			binOp or
			unaryOp or
			cmp or
			binOpImm or
			cmpImm or
			condBranch or
			branch or
			ret or
			param or
			call or
			retVoid or
			globalAddr or
			loadIndirect or
			storeIndirect or
			osAllocPages or
			osWriteStdout or
			rdataAddr or
			tryCall or
			errorReturn or
			osExit or
			funcAddr or
			callIndirect or
			osPanic or
			osReadClock or
			osProcessSpawn or
			osProcessExitCode or
			osCloseHandle or
			osWaitHandle or
			osWaitChild or
			osFreePages or
			switch or
			witnessCall or
			osPipeCreate or
			osEventCreate or
			osThreadCreate or
			osLockInit or
			osLockEnter or
			osLockLeave or
			osReadOverlapped or
			osLastError or
			osProcessSpawnInherit or
			osCancelIo or
			osGetPid or
			osPipeCreateOutbound or
			osWriteFile or
			osReadWallClock or
			witnessTryCall or
			osFileOpenRead or
			osFileClose or
			osReadFile or
			osFileOpenWrite or
			osFileSize or
			osFileDelete or
			osFileRename or
			osFileFindFirst or
			osFileFindClose or
			osFileFindNext or
			osDirCreate or
			osDirRemove or
			osDirCurrentPath or
			osFileAttributes or
			osPanicValue or
			osCommandLine or
			osStdHandle or
			osExecutablePath or
			osWriteStderr or
			stackRecordAddr or
			osOpenNamedSegment or
			osMapNamedSegment or
			osTickCountMs or
			osHandleSetInherit or
			osPipePeek or
			osEnvRead or
			osProcessTerminate or
			osSocketStartup or
			osSocketResolve or
			osSocketFreeAddrInfo or
			osSocketCreate or
			osSocketConnect or
			osSocketSend or
			osSocketError or
			osSocketRecv or
			osSocketClose or
			osCpuCount or
			osThreadCpuTicks or
			osDecommitPages or
			memFill or
			osCommitPages or
			osReservePages or
			atomicCas or
			atomicRmw or
			currentThreadToken or
			tlsAlloc or
			tlsSetValue or
			tlsSlotLoad or
			osSetEvent or
			osEnterBackgroundPriority or
			osFileOpenWriteExecutable or
			osEnvBlock or
			osEnvBlockFree or
			loadAcquire or
			storeRelease or
			osHandleFileType or
			osProcessProbe or
			osCreateNamedSegment or
			osUnmapNamedSegment or
			osUnpublishNamedSegment or
			covPoint or
			osHostSample or
			osNetpoll or
			osPathQuery or
			osSocketOverlapped or
			osSocketServe or
			osDebugHost then rebuilt.push(opIdx)
		div(resultId, lhs, rhs, signedness) then reduceOneDivision(module, opIdx: opIdx, resultId: resultId, dividend: lhs, divisorId: rhs, signedness: signedness, isRemainder: false, constMap: constMap, reduction: reduction, magic: magic, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt, detached: detached)
		mod(resultId, lhs, rhs, signedness) then reduceOneDivision(module, opIdx: opIdx, resultId: resultId, dividend: lhs, divisorId: rhs, signedness: signedness, isRemainder: true, constMap: constMap, reduction: reduction, magic: magic, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt, detached: detached)
	end 'site'
end 'appendReducedOp'

// One `div`/`mod`: classify it, and either keep it or emit its sequence.
function reduceOneDivision(module StdModule, opIdx OpIndex, resultId ValueId, dividend ValueId, divisorId ValueId, signedness IntegerSignedness, isRemainder bool, constMap ConstValueMap, reduction DivisionReduction, magic DivisionMagic, mint ValueMinter, rebuilt OpIndexArray, detached ValueIdArray)
	if not constMap.contains(divisorId) 'divisorIsNotAConstant'
		rebuilt.push(opIdx)
		return
	end 'divisorIsNotAConstant'

	classifyDivision(constMap.constantOf(divisorId), signedness: signedness, isRemainder: isRemainder, magic: magic, out: reduction)

	if not reduction.isReducible() 'keepTheDivide'
		rebuilt.push(opIdx)
		return
	end 'keepTheDivide'

	detached.push(divisorId)

	// The shift/multiply chain IS the division, so it answers to the division's own statement.
	module.spans.current = module.spans.at(opIdx)

	if isRemainder 'remainder'
		emitReducedRemainder(module, resultId: resultId, dividend: dividend, reduction: reduction, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
		return
	end 'remainder'

	emitReducedQuotient(module, resultId: resultId, dividend: dividend, reduction: reduction, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
end 'reduceOneDivision'

// ==================================================================================================
// The emitted sequences
// ==================================================================================================

// `resultId = dividend / K`. The chain's LAST op defines `resultId`, so every reader of the division —
// a range-check guard among them (`insertRangeChecks` has already run, and its `cmp` names this value)
// — goes on naming the value it named before.
function emitReducedQuotient(module StdModule, resultId ValueId, dividend ValueId, reduction DivisionReduction, mint ValueMinter, rebuilt OpIndexArray)
	// A negated quotient needs one more op after the chain, so the chain itself lands in a temporary.
	let chainResult = mint.mint() if reduction.negateQuotient else resultId

	match reduction.kind 'shape'
		notReducible then panic("emitReducedQuotient: reached with a division `classifyDivision` refused — `reduceOneDivision` tests `isReducible` first")
		powerOfTwo then emitPowerOfTwoQuotient(module, resultId: chainResult, dividend: dividend, reduction: reduction, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
		magicMultiply then emitMagicQuotient(module, resultId: chainResult, dividend: dividend, reduction: reduction, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
	end 'shape'

	if reduction.negateQuotient 'negate'
		rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.unaryOp(resultId, operand: chainResult, opcode: StdUnaryOpcode.neg, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))
	end 'negate'
end 'emitReducedQuotient'

// ⭐ `resultId = dividend mod K`, and the general form is `x - (x / |K|) * |K|` — ONE rule, derived
// from the quotient rather than from a second sequence of its own. Two things follow from deriving it:
// the remainder can never disagree with the quotient about a sign or an edge, and where a program
// computes BOTH (`digit = n mod 10` beside `n = n / 10`, which is what every decimal renderer does)
// `commonSubexpressionElimination` collapses the two quotient chains into one.
//
// ⚠ Neither the multiply nor the subtraction can overflow: `|q * |K||` is at most `|x|` because `q` is
// a truncation, and `|x - q * |K||` is the remainder, which is below `|K|`.
//
// ⭐ **THE ONE SHAPE NOT DERIVED FROM A QUOTIENT IS AN UNSIGNED POWER OF TWO**, and it earns its own
// arm: `x modu 2^k` IS the low `k` bits of `x`, which is ONE `and` against a mask where the derived
// form would be a shift, a multiply and a subtract. MEASURED on `fmt.maxon`, this is the commonest
// reduced remainder there is (`x mod 2` in a halving loop).
function emitReducedRemainder(module StdModule, resultId ValueId, dividend ValueId, reduction DivisionReduction, mint ValueMinter, rebuilt OpIndexArray)
	if reduction.isUnsigned and reduction.kind == DivisionReductionKind.powerOfTwo 'lowBitsMask'
		let mask = emitReducedConst(module, value: reduction.divisorMagnitude - 1, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
		rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(resultId, lhs: dividend, rhs: mask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))
		return
	end 'lowBitsMask'

	let quotient = mint.mint()
	emitReducedQuotient(module, resultId: quotient, dividend: dividend, reduction: reduction, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)

	let divisorValue = emitReducedConst(module, value: reduction.divisorMagnitude, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
	let product = mint.mint()
	rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(product, lhs: quotient, rhs: divisorValue, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))
	rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(resultId, lhs: dividend, rhs: product, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))
end 'emitReducedRemainder'

// ⭐⭐ **DIVISION BY `2^k`, AND THE BIAS IS THE WHOLE CONTENT OF IT.** An arithmetic right shift rounds
// toward NEGATIVE INFINITY (`-7 shr 1` is `-4`) while the language's `/` truncates toward ZERO
// (`-7 / 2` is `-3`), so a bare shift is off by one for every negative dividend the divisor does not
// divide exactly. Adding `2^k - 1` before the shift is what corrects it, and the addend is produced
// from the dividend's own sign so that a non-negative dividend adds nothing:
//
//     sgn  = x shr 63             ; arithmetic: all ones iff x < 0, else 0
//     bias = sgn shrLogical 64-k  ; 2^k - 1 iff x < 0, else 0
//     q    = (x + bias) shr k
//
// UNSIGNED needs none of it: there is no negative dividend to correct, so `x /u 2^k` is one logical
// shift — the cheapest reduction in this file, and some thirty cycles cheaper than the `div` it
// replaces.
function emitPowerOfTwoQuotient(module StdModule, resultId ValueId, dividend ValueId, reduction DivisionReduction, mint ValueMinter, rebuilt OpIndexArray)
	if reduction.isUnsigned 'logicalShift'
		let count = emitReducedConst(module, value: reduction.shift as ParsedInt, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
		rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(resultId, lhs: dividend, rhs: count, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))
		return
	end 'logicalShift'

	let signBroadcast = mint.mint()
	let signBitCount = emitReducedConst(module, value: SignBitIndex, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
	rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(signBroadcast, lhs: dividend, rhs: signBitCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))

	let bias = mint.mint()
	let biasCount = emitReducedConst(module, value: ((ReducedWordBits - reduction.shift) as ParsedInt), mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
	rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(bias, lhs: signBroadcast, rhs: biasCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))

	let biased = mint.mint()
	rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(biased, lhs: dividend, rhs: bias, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))

	let quotientCount = emitReducedConst(module, value: reduction.shift as ParsedInt, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
	rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(resultId, lhs: biased, rhs: quotientCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))
end 'emitPowerOfTwoQuotient'

// ⭐⭐ **DIVISION BY A GENERAL CONSTANT: MULTIPLY BY THE RECIPROCAL, IN FIXED POINT.** `M / 2^(64+s)`
// approximates `1 / K` closely enough that the high half of `x * M`, shifted right by `s`, is exactly
// `floor(x / K)` for every 64-bit `x` — which each derivation's exit condition is the proof of. The
// high multiply is the half both signednesses share; the fixups that follow it are not, and are the two
// tails below.
//
// The magic goes on the LEFT of the multiply deliberately: `StdToX64Conversion.lowerMulHigh`
// moves the left operand into RAX, and a `movRegImm` whose only reader is that move is biased into RAX
// by `collectOpHints`, so the copy self-elides. (`foldConstOperands` cannot reorder the two operands:
// it swaps a commutative op's operands only when it is folding one into an immediate, and
// `constFitsBinOpImmediate` refuses every constant for this opcode.)
function emitMagicQuotient(module StdModule, resultId ValueId, dividend ValueId, reduction DivisionReduction, mint ValueMinter, rebuilt OpIndexArray)
	let multiplier = emitReducedConst(module, value: reduction.magic, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
	let highOpcode = StdBinOpcode.mulHighUnsigned if reduction.isUnsigned else StdBinOpcode.mulHighSigned

	let highProduct = mint.mint()
	rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(highProduct, lhs: multiplier, rhs: dividend, opcode: highOpcode, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))

	if reduction.isUnsigned 'unsignedTail'
		emitUnsignedMagicTail(module, resultId: resultId, dividend: dividend, highProduct: highProduct, reduction: reduction, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
		return
	end 'unsignedTail'

	emitSignedMagicTail(module, resultId: resultId, dividend: dividend, highProduct: highProduct, reduction: reduction, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
end 'emitMagicQuotient'

//     h = mulhi_u(M, x)
//     h = ((x - h) shrLogical 1) + h   ; ONLY when `addIndicator` — the 65th bit of the reciprocal
//     q = h shrLogical s
//
// ⚠ **THE FIXUP IS WRITTEN AS `((x - h) >> 1) + h` AND NOT AS THE `(x + h) >> 1` IT IS EQUAL TO**, and
// the difference is the whole of why it is correct: `x + h` is a 65-bit sum for a large dividend and
// there is no 65th bit to hold it, while `x - h` never borrows (`h` is at most `x` here) and halving it
// before the add keeps every intermediate inside the word. The shorter spelling drops the carry and
// answers a wrong quotient for every dividend whose sum overflows.
function emitUnsignedMagicTail(module StdModule, resultId ValueId, dividend ValueId, highProduct ValueId, reduction DivisionReduction, mint ValueMinter, rebuilt OpIndexArray)
	if reduction.shift == 0 'noScaleToApply'
		panic("emitUnsignedMagicTail: a post-shift of 0 — `deriveUnsignedMagic` answers `floor(log2 K)`, which is at least 1 for every divisor that reaches it, because a power of two takes `emitPowerOfTwoQuotient` instead")
	end 'noScaleToApply'

	var scaled = highProduct

	if reduction.addIndicator 'restoreTheImplicitBit'
		let difference = mint.mint()
		rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(difference, lhs: dividend, rhs: highProduct, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))

		let halvingCount = emitReducedConst(module, value: AddIndicatorHalvingShift, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
		let halved = mint.mint()
		rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(halved, lhs: difference, rhs: halvingCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))

		let recombined = mint.mint()
		rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(recombined, lhs: halved, rhs: highProduct, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))
		scaled = recombined
	end 'restoreTheImplicitBit'

	let scaleCount = emitReducedConst(module, value: reduction.shift as ParsedInt, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
	rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(resultId, lhs: scaled, rhs: scaleCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))
end 'emitUnsignedMagicTail'

//     h = h + x                    ; ONLY when M's true value was at least 2^63 (see below)
//     h = h shr s                  ; omitted when s is 0
//     q = h + (h shrLogical 63)    ; +1 for a negative h — floor becomes truncate
//
// ⚠ **THE `+ x` IS NOT AN OPTIONAL OPTIMIZATION, IT IS PART OF THE MULTIPLIER.** The magic is a 64-bit
// UNSIGNED number and may legitimately have its top bit set; the machine's `imul` reads it as SIGNED,
// i.e. as `M - 2^64`, so the product is short by exactly `x * 2^64` — whose high half is `x`. Adding
// the dividend back is what restores it. `magicIsNegative` is that condition, and it is derived from
// the sign of the stored multiplier rather than carried as a separate flag, so the two cannot disagree.
function emitSignedMagicTail(module StdModule, resultId ValueId, dividend ValueId, highProduct ValueId, reduction DivisionReduction, mint ValueMinter, rebuilt OpIndexArray)
	var high = highProduct

	if reduction.magicIsNegative 'restoreTheWrappedMultiplier'
		let corrected = mint.mint()
		rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(corrected, lhs: high, rhs: dividend, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))
		high = corrected
	end 'restoreTheWrappedMultiplier'

	if reduction.shift > 0 'scaleDown'
		let shifted = mint.mint()
		let scaleCount = emitReducedConst(module, value: reduction.shift as ParsedInt, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
		rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(shifted, lhs: high, rhs: scaleCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))
		high = shifted
	end 'scaleDown'

	let signBit = mint.mint()
	let signBitCount = emitReducedConst(module, value: SignBitIndex, mint: mint, rebuilt: rebuilt)
	rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(signBit, lhs: high, rhs: signBitCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))

	rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.binOp(resultId, lhs: high, rhs: signBit, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: ReducedOperandType)))
end 'emitSignedMagicTail'

// A fresh `const` of `value`, appended to the rebuilt op list, answering the value it defines.
//
// ⚠ Every constant this pass emits is FRESH rather than a reuse of the divisor's own `const`, and both
// halves of that are deliberate: the magic and the shift counts are numbers the divisor's op does not
// hold, and the multiply-back wants `|K|` where the op holds `K`. `foldConstOperands` then folds each
// of them INTO the op that reads it, so none survives as an instruction unless its value is too wide
// for the immediate field — which for the 64-bit magic is always, and for every shift count never.
function emitReducedConst(module StdModule, value ParsedInt, mint ValueMinter, rebuilt OpIndexArray) returns ValueId
	let id = mint.mint()
	rebuilt.push(appendReducedSequenceOp(module, op: StdOp.const(id, value: value, valueType: ReducedOperandType)))
	return id
end 'emitReducedConst'

// Append `op` to the module's flat op array and answer its index. Blocks reference ops by index, so a
// new op is created here and referenced from the rebuilt `opRefs` rather than appended to a block —
// which would place it at the block's END rather than at the division's position.
// (`PromoteStackRecords.appendModuleOp` is the same three lines for the same reason; it is a private
// `function` there, so this file states them rather than reaching across.)
function appendReducedSequenceOp(module StdModule, op StdOp) returns OpIndex
	let opIdx = module.ops.count()
	module.ops.push(op)
	module.spans.pushCurrent(opIdx)
	return opIdx
end 'appendReducedSequenceOp'

// Drop the divisor `const` of every rewritten site that NOTHING now reads.
//
// ⚠ **MEMBERSHIP IS RE-DERIVED FROM THE REWRITTEN FUNCTION, NOT COUNTED DURING THE REWRITE, AND THAT
// IS WHAT MAKES IT EXACT.** A divisor `const` may be read by two divisions this pass reduced, by one
// it reduced and one it refused, or by something else entirely; a per-site counter would have to model
// all three. Asking `collectFunctionValueUses` — StdDialect's one answer to "every value this function
// reads" — of the function as it stands after the rewrite answers all three at once, and is the same walk
// `foldConstOperands`' own prune makes.
//
// It retires ONLY the consts this pass detached, which is `foldConstOperands`' narrow rule verbatim: a
// general dead-value DCE is its own pass, and `foldConstOperands`' header states at length why this
// must not grow into one.
function retireDetachedDivisors(module StdModule, func IrFunction, detached ValueIdArray)
	if detached.count() == 0 'nothingDetached'
		return
	end 'nothingDetached'

	var stillRead = StdValueUseSet.create()
	collectFunctionValueUses(module, func: func, referenced: stillRead)

	var doomed = StdValueUseSet.create()

	for valueId in detached 'eachDetached'
		if not stillRead.contains(valueId) 'unread'
			doomed.insert(valueId)
		end 'unread'
	end 'eachDetached'

	removeOpsDefining(module, func: func, doomed: doomed)
end 'retireDetachedDivisors'
